{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: New Deal for Working People (ban exploitative zero‑hours contracts, end fire‑and‑rehire, day‑one rights, genuine living wage); NHS commitments (40,000 extra weekly appointments, 8,500 extra mental health staff, Dentistry Rescue Plan, National Care Service); halve violence against women and girls with specialist units and fast‑track rape courts; Warm Homes Plan plus clean up rivers, restore nature, stronger animal welfare; housing push (1.5m homes, more social and affordable homes, end Section 21 evictions, homelessness strategy); strict fiscal rules with OBR oversight, no rises in NI/Income Tax/VAT, and closing non‑dom/offshore/carry loopholes; strong NATO and nuclear deterrent, Border Security Command and fast‑track returns. TEXT_F: Halve employee NI and abolish main rate NI for the self‑employed; welfare reform with tougher sanctions and new Fraud Bill; legal annual cap on migration and monthly Rwanda removal flights; Mandatory National Service and tougher sentences; protect single‑sex spaces and define sex in law as biological; NHS funding up each year with more nurses/doctors and a Dental Recovery Plan; 30 hours’ free childcare from nine months; 1.6m homes with Help to Buy and Stamp Duty relief; defence to 2.5% of GDP; maintain price cap, PumpWatch, reverse London ULEZ; annual North Sea oil and gas licensing.",
  "reasoning": "Because the electorate’s top priorities are Care and Equality, TEXT_E more directly emphasizes reducing harm and expanding fair treatment through extensive NHS, social care, violence‑reduction, worker‑rights, childcare/schools, housing and environmental health measures. While TEXT_F speaks strongly to Proportionality, Loyalty and Authority via tax cuts, welfare sanctions, migration caps and National Service, TEXT_E still addresses proportionality with fiscal rules and tax‑loophole closures and pairs this with credible security commitments, yielding closer overall alignment to the profile weights.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
